162	// nestedRepoEvidencePaths returns, for the repoRoot-relative kept paths, one
163	// absolute REPRESENTATIVE path per distinct git repo NESTED under repoRoot —
164	// a `.git` entry (dir or gitfile: linked worktrees and submodules use
165	// gitfiles) strictly between a path's directory and repoRoot. Detection is
166	// stat-only; the returned paths are handed to the budgeted git resolver for
167	// canonical (innermost) worktree-root/common-dir resolution, so a false
168	// positive (stale .git junk) costs one fork and is then discarded by the
169	// resolver or the own-worktree skip. Emitting per detected nested ROOT (not
170	// per kept directory) matters: a turn editing many directories of one busy
171	// nested repo must cost the resolver one fork for that repo, not spend the
172	// whole per-turn resolution budget re-resolving it — which would also starve
173	// a second nested repo appearing later in the same turn.
174	//
175	// Nested repos registered as submodules in repoRoot's .gitmodules are part of
176	// the parent project and excluded — including repos UNDER a registered
177	// submodule path (a submodule's own submodules are transitively part of the
178	// parent project; prefix containment handles that without parsing every
179	// submodule's .gitmodules). Unregistered ones (the dotfiles/home-repo case)
180	// are evidence. Relative junk that escapes repoRoot after joining
181	// (../ traversal kept by the clamp) is skipped: it was never resolvable
182	// evidence.
183	func nestedRepoEvidencePaths(repoRoot string, keptRelGroups [][]string) []string {
